class MyQueue {
public:
    stack<int> s1, s2;

    MyQueue() {}

    void push(int x) {
        while (!s1.empty()) {
            s2.push(s1.top());
            s1.pop();
        }

        s1.push(x);

        while (!s2.empty()) {
            s1.push(s2.top());
            s2.pop();
        }
    }

    int pop() {
        int x = s1.top();
        s1.pop();
        return x;
    }

    int peek() {
        return s1.top();
    }

    bool empty() {
        return s1.empty();
    }
};

**Intuition:**  
Use two stacks to simulate FIFO behavior.  
Keep the front element accessible at the top of `s1`.

**Algorithm:**  
For `push`, move elements to `s2`, insert the new element, then move them back.  
`pop` and `peek` operate on the top of `s1`.

**Complexity:**  
Time: **O(n)** for `push`, **O(1)** for `pop/peek`  
Space: **O(n)**
